--[[
The lane-parallel half of the AOT IR, and what makes it valid.

An `@aot` body of one top-level numeric map loop may run several iterations at
once. This is the vocabulary that rewrite produces and the rule for what counts
as a well-formed instance of it.

There is one record per operation and `Expr` is their union, discriminated by
`op`, exactly as the concrete syntax tree is discriminated by `kind`. That is the
point of writing it here rather than as untyped tables: a pass that dispatches on
`op` is checked against the vocabulary rather than trusted with it, and an
operation whose operands are the wrong width stops being something a verifier has
to remember to look for.

Generated C is a backend representation and not the safety boundary. Every span
access, region relationship, conversion, and lane operation has to exist here,
verified, before anything is emitted -- so this module answers to the ownership
and alias facts the checker proved, and invents none of its own.

A vector type is written `<element>x<lanes>`: `f64x4` is four binary64 lanes,
`m32x8` is a mask over eight 32-bit ones. Every gang is 32 bytes wide, so the
lane count follows the element width and the two are never independent.
]]

local scalarIR = require("nupp.compiler.aot.scalar")

local aotLane = {}

--- Where an operation came from, for a diagnostic that has to name it.
---
--- The scalar half's, rather than a second record of the same shape: lane IR
--- embeds scalar uniforms, so it already depends on that vocabulary, and two
--- structurally identical positions would be different types that no conversion
--- exists between.
type aotLane.Source = scalarIR.Source

--- A vector type name. Not an enumeration: a shape names the vectors it uses and
--- the verifier checks membership against that shape, so a width one target
--- admits is not silently valid on another.
type aotLane.Vector = string

--- The element types a lane may carry.
---
--- `i32` and `u32` are the same lanes and differ only in the operations applied
--- to them, and `bool` is not an element at all -- it is what a comparison
--- produces. Both are here because a field's storage type is looked up directly:
--- collapsing them would mean the lookup had to translate first, and a lookup
--- that translates is a place the two halves can disagree about a width.
type aotLane.Element = 'f64' | 'f32' | 'i32' | 'u32' | 'bool'

--- One gang: how many iterations run at once, and which vector type each element
--- is carried in. Within one width, a binary64 gang and a 32-bit gang cost the
--- same registers and differ only in how many iterations they hold.
record aotLane.Shape
    --- The name this shape is known by, and what a diagnostic calls it.
    name: string

    --- Iterations per group.
    lanes: integer

    --- How wide one of its vectors is, in bytes.
    ---
    --- What decides whether a target can hold this gang at all. A vector wider
    --- than the widest register class the target has is not a slower gang, it is
    --- not a gang: it has no stable ABI at a function boundary, and the compiler
    --- says so.
    bytes: integer

    --- The mask vector. One lane per iteration, all bits set or none.
    mask: aotLane.Vector

    --- The vector each element type is carried in. An element absent from here
    --- cannot enter this gang at all, which is how a binary64 value keeps a
    --- 32-bit gang from taking a loop it would change the answers of.
    vectorFor: {[aotLane.Element]: aotLane.Vector}

    --- Where bitwise work happens. A gang carrying integers in binary64 lanes
    --- converts out to this and back, which changes nothing because every
    --- uint32 is an exact binary64 value.
    bits: aotLane.Vector

    --- The mask a comparison of that element produces, where it is not the
    --- gang's own.
    ---
    --- A comparison yields one bit per lane as wide as the values compared, so a
    --- gang carrying elements at several widths produces masks at several
    --- widths. Everything the mask algebra does -- and, not, testing whether any
    --- lane is live -- happens at the gang's own `mask`, and a comparison that
    --- produced a different one converts to it immediately. Converting at the
    --- two boundaries keeps one width in the middle, where a mask typed per
    --- element everywhere would have every combination of two masks asking which
    --- width it was.
    ---
    --- Absent for a gang that carries everything in one width, which is every
    --- gang that is not mixed.
    maskFor: {[aotLane.Element]: aotLane.Vector}?

    --- The vector an element is rounded through when this gang carries it wider
    --- than it is.
    ---
    --- A gang carrying binary32 in binary64 lanes can still perform an explicit
    --- binary32 operation: compute it in the wider lanes and round the result
    --- once to binary32. That is bit-identical to the native single-precision
    --- instruction by the same argument the scalar lowering rests on -- 53 is at
    --- least twice 24 plus 2 -- so it costs an instruction rather than an answer.
    ---
    --- Without it a loop mixing explicit binary32 with one binary64 value gets no
    --- gang at all: the 32-bit gang refuses the binary64 value and the binary64
    --- gang refuses the explicit binary32 operation.
    rounding: {[aotLane.Element]: aotLane.Vector}?
end

--- One mask at another mask's width.
---
--- Masks are all-ones or all-zeros per lane, so widening is a sign extension and
--- narrowing is a truncation, and both preserve exactly those two values. That
--- is why this is a conversion rather than a shuffle, and why it costs one
--- instruction.
record aotLane.MaskCast
    op: 'vmask_cast'

    --- The mask being converted.
    args: {aotLane.Expr}

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- One value at another lane width.
---
--- A gang that carries every element in one vector needs none of these: a
--- binary32 value already sat in the binary64 lanes a binary64 consumer wanted.
--- A gang that carries each element at its own width has to convert where the
--- two meet, and the conversion is the arithmetic one -- widening is exact,
--- narrowing rounds once, which is what the source said either way.
record aotLane.Convert
    op: 'vconvert'

    --- The value being converted.
    args: {aotLane.Expr}

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A uniform value broadcast to every lane.
record aotLane.Splat
    op: 'vsplat'

    --- The scalar expression, in the scalar IR's own vocabulary.
    args: {scalarIR.Expr}

    --- The element the scalar was brought to before broadcasting.
    element: aotLane.Element

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A uniform boolean broadcast to a mask: all bits set, or none.
record aotLane.BoolSplat
    op: 'vbool_splat'
    args: {any}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- One field of consecutive span elements, gathered into a vector.
---
--- Consecutive is the whole of it: the index must be the loop's own, so no lane
--- reads an element another lane wrote and nothing has to prove it did not.
record aotLane.FieldLoad
    op: 'vfield_load'

    --- The span parameter read from.
    span: string

    --- The reified struct its elements are.
    layout: string

    --- The field within that struct.
    field: string

    --- How many consecutive elements, which is the gang's lane count.
    lanes: integer

    --- The field's storage type, which decides the vector it loads into.
    scalarType: aotLane.Element

    --- The physical field storage, which may be narrower than its value.
    sourceType: string?

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Consecutive scalar span elements gathered into one vector.
record aotLane.SpanLoad
    op: 'vspan_load'
    span: string
    lanes: integer
    scalarType: aotLane.Element
    sourceType: string?
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- The elementwise verbs a gang's arithmetic and comparisons are written with.
type aotLane.BinaryVerb = 'add' | 'sub' | 'mul' | 'div' | 'lt' | 'le' | 'gt' | 'ge' | 'eq' | 'ne'

--- Elementwise arithmetic, or an elementwise comparison producing a mask.
---
--- The operation names its own vector type -- `vadd.f64x4`, `vlt.f32x8` -- so
--- there is no operation whose operand width has to be inferred, and a mismatched
--- pair is a malformed opcode rather than a check somebody has to remember.
record aotLane.Binary
    --- One tag, with the verb and the width as fields.
    ---
    --- The opcode used to carry both -- `vadd.f64x4` -- which reads well and
    --- cannot be dispatched on: narrowing a union needs a literal tag, and a
    --- generated string is not one. Nor can a consumer test the record
    --- nominally, because `is` compares metatables and IR arriving from outside
    --- has none. Comparing this tag does both.
    op: 'vbinary'

    --- What the operation does.
    verb: aotLane.BinaryVerb

    args: {aotLane.Expr}

    --- The element the operands are, which is not the result type for a
    --- comparison.
    element: aotLane.Element

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Elementwise negation or square root.
record aotLane.Unary
    op: 'vunary'

    verb: 'neg' | 'sqrt'
    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    element: aotLane.Element
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Mask combination: `vmand`, `vmor`, `vmnot`.
record aotLane.MaskOp
    op: 'vmask'

    verb: 'and' | 'or' | 'not'
    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A short-circuit `and` or `or`, evaluated on both sides.
---
--- Admissible only with a proof that the right side is pure and total, because
--- both sides run for every lane. `effect` carries that proof rather than the
--- pass inferring it from the shape of the expression, which would make a future
--- effectful right side quietly eager.
record aotLane.ShortCircuit
    op: 'vshort'

    verb: 'and' | 'or'
    args: {aotLane.Expr}

    --- The proof. Only `pure_total` admits eager evaluation.
    effect: 'pure_total'

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Per-lane choice. The inactive lanes keep what they had, which is what makes a
--- conditional a mask rather than a branch.
---
--- It has to be a real select. Multiplying by a zero mask is not one: an escaped
--- lane's value overflows to infinity, and zero times infinity is NaN, so the
--- lane that was supposed to be frozen is destroyed instead.
record aotLane.Select
    op: 'vselect'

    --- Mask, then the value for active lanes, then the value for inactive ones.
    args: {aotLane.Expr}

    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A closed math operation, one scalar call per lane.
record aotLane.Math
    op: 'vmath'

    --- Which operation, from the admitted closed set.
    intrinsic: string

    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A binary32 operation whose native instruction is right about the value and
--- wrong about NaN, expanded through the helper that repairs it.
---
--- `min`, `max` and `fma` are the three. A differential over every interesting
--- binary32 value found they disagree with `fminf`, `fmaxf` and `fmaf` in
--- exactly two respects: `nupp.math.f32` canonicalizes every NaN where the
--- instruction propagates a payload, and min and max answer with that canonical
--- NaN where IEEE `minNum` answers with whichever operand is not NaN.
record aotLane.Corrected
    op: 'vcorrected'

    --- The C helper that performs the correction, shared with the scalar body so
    --- the two cannot be right in one form and wrong in the other.
    helper: string

    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    element: aotLane.Element
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A bitwise operation on flag words: `vbit.<verb>.<vector>`.
---
--- An entity query is made of these, so they lower rather than refusing the
--- loop. Shift counts are masked and a logical shift stays unsigned, as the
--- scalar helpers do.
record aotLane.Bitwise
    op: 'vbitwise'

    verb: 'and' | 'or' | 'xor' | 'shl' | 'shr' | 'sar' | 'not'
    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Conversion into or out of the gang's bit vector. Exact in both directions,
--- because every value it converts is exactly representable on both sides.
record aotLane.BitConvert
    --- Only the binary64 gang converts: a 32-bit gang already carries integers
    --- in the vector its bitwise work happens in, so there is nothing to cross.
    op: 'vbits'

    --- Into the bit vector, or back out of it.
    direction: 'to' | 'from'

    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Rounding a value to a narrower element and carrying it on in the wider one.
---
--- The only lane operation that changes a value without changing its vector.
--- What it costs is one conversion each way; what it buys is that an explicit
--- fixed-width operation can happen in a gang that carries the value wider,
--- which is the difference between four lanes and none for a loop that mixes
--- widths.
record aotLane.Round
    op: 'vround'

    --- The element the value is rounded to. The vector it is carried in does not
    --- change, which is what makes this a rounding rather than a conversion.
    element: aotLane.Element

    --- The vector it goes out through, carried rather than looked up so the
    --- emitter reads one node instead of the node and the gang.
    narrow: aotLane.Vector

    args: {aotLane.Expr}
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A lane local: a vector or mask bound earlier in this block.
record aotLane.Local
    op: 'local'
    name: string
    cName: string
    type: aotLane.Vector
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- The gangs this backend chooses between.
---
--- Two widths, and within each width a binary64 gang and a 32-bit one. Ordinary
--- Nupp arithmetic is binary64, so a loop written with operators takes the
--- binary64 gang; one whose values the source established as `float` and `int32`
--- through `nupp.math` takes the 32-bit one and gets twice the lanes for the
--- same registers.
---
--- The 16-byte pair exists because the widest vector a target has a register
--- class for is not always 32 bytes. x86-64 without AVX has 16, and until these
--- were here that meant no lanes at all rather than half as many -- which made
--- the most common target the one the lowering did nothing for.
---
--- Selection is all-or-nothing within a width: a single binary64 varying value
--- drops the whole loop to the binary64 gang. The rule it stands in for is a
--- gang size chosen from aggregate register pressure, with each value occupying
--- however many registers its element needs, which wants mask widths to convert.
const aotLane.SHAPES = {
    new aotLane.Shape(
        name = "mixed4",
        lanes = 4,
        bytes = 32,
        mask = "m64x4",
        -- Integers ride in binary64 lanes because every int32 and uint32 is an
        -- exact binary64 value, which is also what ordinary Nupp does with them.
        -- Binary32 does not: it rode there too until measuring showed what that
        -- cost, and it has its own lanes now.
        vectorFor = {["f64"] = "f64x4", ["f32"] = "f32x4", ["i32"] = "f64x4", ["u32"] = "f64x4", ["bool"] = "m64x4"},
        maskFor = {["f32"] = "m32x4"},
        bits = "u32x4"
    ),
    new aotLane.Shape(
        name = "f32x8",
        lanes = 8,
        bytes = 32,
        mask = "m32x8",
        -- No `f64` entry: a binary64 varying value cannot enter a 32-bit gang,
        -- and narrowing one here would change an answer the source did not.
        vectorFor = {["f32"] = "f32x8", ["i32"] = "i32x8", ["u32"] = "i32x8", ["bool"] = "m32x8"},
        bits = "i32x8"
    ),
    new aotLane.Shape(
        name = "mixed2",
        lanes = 2,
        bytes = 16,
        mask = "m64x2",
        vectorFor = {["f64"] = "f64x2", ["f32"] = "f32x2", ["i32"] = "f64x2", ["u32"] = "f64x2", ["bool"] = "m64x2"},
        maskFor = {["f32"] = "m32x2"},
        bits = "u32x2"
    ),
    new aotLane.Shape(
        name = "f32x4",
        lanes = 4,
        bytes = 16,
        mask = "m32x4",
        vectorFor = {["f32"] = "f32x4", ["i32"] = "i32x4", ["u32"] = "i32x4", ["bool"] = "m32x4"},
        bits = "i32x4"
    ),
}

--- The same gangs by name, for a body that says which one it was lowered to.
const aotLane.SHAPE_BY_NAME = (function(): {[string]: aotLane.Shape}
    local byName: {[string]: aotLane.Shape} = {}
    for _, shape in ipairs(aotLane.SHAPES) do
        byName[shape.name] = shape
    end

    return byName
end)()

--- Below this many arithmetic operations per byte a loop body touches, lane
--- lowering is declined.
---
--- Lane lowering is a large win where a loop stays in registers and a large loss
--- where it streams memory: measured against their own scalar bodies, Mandelbrot
--- runs about twice its scalar speed with four lanes while a component update
--- runs between a tenth and four fifths of its. Neither layout nor element width
--- moves that, so what separates them is how much arithmetic there is to
--- amortize assembling and taking apart the vectors.
---
--- The kernels this was calibrated against sit either side of a gap of more than
--- ten times, so the number is round rather than tuned. It is an estimate
--- standing in for a measurement, which is why the source overrides it in either
--- direction.
const aotLane.INTENSITY_THRESHOLD = 1.0

--- What operations inside a data-dependent inner loop count for.
---
--- They run many times per iteration of the loop being lowered and the trip
--- count is not a static fact, so this stands in for that count rather than
--- claiming it.
const aotLane.INNER_LOOP_WEIGHT = 8

--- Every lane-typed expression.
---
--- A scalar expression may appear where a uniform value is wanted; it is not in
--- this union, and the verifier hands it to the scalar rules instead.
type aotLane.Expr = aotLane.Splat
    | aotLane.BoolSplat
    | aotLane.FieldLoad
    | aotLane.SpanLoad
    | aotLane.Binary
    | aotLane.Unary
    | aotLane.MaskOp
    | aotLane.ShortCircuit
    | aotLane.Select
    | aotLane.Math
    | aotLane.Corrected
    | aotLane.Bitwise
    | aotLane.BitConvert
    | aotLane.Round
    | aotLane.MaskCast
    | aotLane.Convert
    | aotLane.Local

--- Declaring a lane local.
record aotLane.Let
    op: 'let'
    name: string
    cName: string

    --- A vector where the value varies, and the scalar it already was where it
    --- does not: a uniform binding stays uniform rather than being broadcast
    --- into every lane and read back out of one.
    value: aotLane.Expr | scalarIR.Expr

    type: string
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A lane local being assigned to.
---
--- Two records rather than one with optional fields for each shape: a field
--- that is only sometimes there is one every reader has to check, and the
--- checker will not let it be read as though it were always present.
record aotLane.LocalTarget
    kind: 'local'
    name: string
    cName: string
end

--- One field of consecutive span elements, which is the only place lane IR
--- writes memory.
record aotLane.FieldTarget
    kind: 'vfield'
    span: string
    layout: string
    field: string

    --- The field's storage type, which is what the store narrows to.
    scalarType: aotLane.Element

    sourceType: string?
end

--- Consecutive elements of one writable scalar span.
record aotLane.SpanTarget
    kind: 'vspan'
    span: string
    scalarType: aotLane.Element
    sourceType: string?
end

--- Where one assignment puts its value.
type aotLane.Target = aotLane.LocalTarget | aotLane.FieldTarget | aotLane.SpanTarget

--- One target and the value going into it.
record aotLane.Assignment
    target: aotLane.Target
    value: aotLane.Expr
end

--- Simultaneous assignment, as the source wrote it.
---
--- Every value is computed before any is stored, because `a, b = b, a` means
--- what it says and a lane rewrite that stored as it went would not.
record aotLane.Assign
    op: 'vassign'
    values: {aotLane.Assignment}
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A loop whose trip count differs per lane.
---
--- `live` holds the lanes still in the loop; `executing` holds those still
--- running the current iteration, which is a smaller set as soon as one lane
--- breaks or continues. The loop ends when no lane is live, so a lane that
--- retires early stops contributing without stopping the others.
record aotLane.While
    op: 'vwhile'
    live: any
    executing: any

    --- Which lanes enter the loop at all.
    initial: aotLane.Expr

    --- Re-evaluated at the bottom, narrowing `live`.
    condition: aotLane.Expr

    body: {any}
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A loop every lane runs the same number of times.
---
--- Its condition is uniform and nothing in it leaves early, so no lane can be
--- at a different iteration from any other and there is nothing for a mask to
--- say. It stays an ordinary loop over lane-parallel statements.
---
--- Worth keeping distinct from the masked form rather than broadcasting the
--- condition into one. The masked form is correct here -- an all-ones mask
--- makes every select and every retirement a no-op -- but it pays a select per
--- assignment and a horizontal test per iteration for an answer that was never
--- in doubt, in the innermost loop of the program.
record aotLane.UniformWhile
    op: 'vwhile_uniform'

    --- Evaluated as an ordinary scalar, because it is one.
    condition: any

    body: {any}
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A `break`, as per-lane retirement.
---
--- The masked lanes leave both the iteration and the loop. Two records rather
--- than one with two tags, because a tag that could be either narrows to
--- neither.
record aotLane.Break
    op: 'vbreak'
    mask: aotLane.Expr
    live: any
    executing: any
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- A `continue`: the masked lanes leave the iteration and stay in the loop.
record aotLane.Continue
    op: 'vcontinue'
    mask: aotLane.Expr
    live: any
    executing: any
    source: aotLane.Source?
end

--- Every lane statement.
type aotLane.Statement = aotLane.Let
    | aotLane.Assign
    | aotLane.While
    | aotLane.UniformWhile
    | aotLane.Break
    | aotLane.Continue

return aotLane
